well right now u.s senators are grilling  senior defense officials about the  withdrawal of military forces from  afghanistan and the collapse of the  afghan government defense secretary  lloyd austin joint chiefs of staff  chairman mark milley and the head of  central command general kenneth mckenzie  are all testifying the biden  administration has been widely accused  of being unprepared for the fall of  kabul senior officials have admitted  that taliban forces advanced far more  quickly than anticipated  we helped build the state mr chairman  but we could not forge a nation  the fact that the afghan army that we  and our partners trained simply melted  away  in many cases without firing a shot took  us all by surprise and it would be  dishonest to claim otherwise  we need to consider some uncomfortable  truths  that we didn't fully comprehend the  depth of corruption and poor leadership  in the senior ranks  that we didn't grasp the damaging effect  affect the frequent and unexplained  rotations by president ghani of his  commanders  that we didn't anticipate the snowball  effect effect caused by the deals that  the taliban commanders struck with local  leaders in a wake of the doha agreement  and that the doha agreement itself had a  demoralizing effect on afghan soldiers  while those proceedings are continuing  right now on capitol hill let's listen  but to the families of those who have  been deployed over and over again into  iraq and afghanistan over the course of  the last 20 years  they are relieved that america is now  turning the page and rejecting the  notion that we should be a nation in  permanent war  second the effort to evacuate more than  120 000 people to safety under chaotic  circumstances was remarkable i visited  the dulles expo center the principal  arrival point for about 80 percent of  the afghans i also visited fort lee the  first of the eight forts that process  afghans and i visited with afghans our  troops the many federal agencies working  together ngos  the competent and compassionate service  on the american side and the deep  gratitude among afghans made a deep  impression on me we should do all we can  to make that transition to safe life in  america as productive as possible  my chief criticism and question is this  why did the afghan security force and  civilian government collapse so quickly  and why did the u.s so overestimate  their capacity  the second half of the question why we  overestimated their capacity is very  important to any who have said we  couldn't see this coming the members of  this committee know that's wrong  an immediate collapse may not have been  the most likely outcome but we have  heard for years particularly from the  intel community that dod estimates of  afghan strength were way too optimistic  i believe the u.s government had a good  evacuation plan but it was premised on  an afghan civilian and military  government that showed high resistance  to the taliban and so we did not  adequately plan for the real possibility  of a quick collapse we need to explore  both military and interagency  decision-making processes to understand  why we were unrealistic and how to  correct that going forward  but the most important part of the  question is why a military we had  trained for 20 years at a cost of 800  plus billion dollars collapsed so  quickly i can think of three reasons but  after i put them on the table i would  like each of you beginning with general  mckenzie to address the question and if  we can't we can do it when we come back  after lunch  first the lightning collapse may show  that our training was insufficient and  that it did not  prepare the afghan military to defend  the country on their own that should  have been our goal but we failed to  accomplish it if so how must we change  our thinking about training foreign  militaries  second the lightning collapse may not  prove that the nsf were poor fighters  but that they were demoralized did they  lack confidence in their own political  and military leaders were they  demoralized by a 2020 peace agreement  between the u.s and the taliban that  didn't even include the afghan  government mr chair i'd like to  introduce the peace agreement for the  record  objection did u.s and allied funding  deepen a culture of corruption that long  predated our involvement even the best  fighting force may give in if they have  no confidence in their leadership  third the lightning collapse may show  that we wanted things for afghans that  afghan leadership did not want for  themselves we celebrated gains in public  health and women's education and we  assumed that afghans would fight to  preserve those gains rather than allow  the taliban to take over in other words  we thought we knew what afghans wanted  what they feared and what they would  fight for  but was our belief though  well-intentioned incredibly naive we  can't get one third of americans to take  a coveted vaccine or accept the results  of a presidential election do we really  think we can transform the culture of  another nation  so to each of our witnesses when we  return in the second round i will ask  you this question why do you believe the  afghan military and civilian government  collapsed so quickly without all your  future well let's bring in our  correspondent patty culhane she's  watching proceedings for us in  washington dc patty some very tough  questions there to answer today  including some as we've been hearing  pointed ones about intelligence failures  and i imagine also about civilian  casualties  they did bring up that drone strike  which when it first happened general  mark milley said was a right to strike  it turned out that it was an aid worker  and ten afghans died including seven  children uh the on intelligence i  thought this was somewhat newsworthy uh  general milley said that the  intelligence was a complete failure and  he pointed to perhaps why he said look  we can point to how many guns and how  many planes and how many helicopters and  humvees we've given them but we don't  know what the hearts of the soldiers are  unless we have advisors on the ground  with them and he said we stopped putting  advisors with them for three years so  another thing he said is one of the  lessons they're learning is perhaps they  try to make the afghan military too much  like the american military trying to  give them high-tech equipment when  perhaps that's not what was needed  as for exactly  how the pentagon though didn't see this  coming the pentagon likes to say they  have a plan for everything we just heard  the secretary of defense admit that they  never tabletop exercises to how they  plan  tabletop exercises that they never had  one where it showed the complete  collapse of the taliban so lots of  pointed questions being asked but the  big one they want to know is how many  americans are still in afghanistan  they're trying to point the finger at  state we just heard from a u.s senator  said no i'm going to need those numbers  by the end of this hearing so a lot of  pointed questions so far a lot of blame  game going on we see people saying that  donald trump made the deal with the  taliban and so it was doomed mission  from that point on and then republicans  are saying look what joe biden was  responsible for the drawdown that was  just so chaotic and some say  embarrassing  pasha calhan there watching those  proceedings for us on capitol hill  thanks so much patty  well meanwhile in afghanistan the  taliban says it's planning to implement  a constitution from nearly six decades  ago now afghanistan's new rulers want to  apply it only where it doesn't conflict  with their version of islamic law the  existing constitution was drawn up with  the help of the u.s back in 2004 but the  taliban says it wants no foreign  interference hashem mahalbara has more  now from kabul  the taliban seem to be trying to  extend an olive branch to the  international community by saying look  we could have resurrected the  constitutional declaration of 1998 when  the taliban were in power here in  afghanistan instead we chose to go for  the 1964 constitution why that  constitution in particular because it's  widely seen as the constitution that  paved the way for constitutional  democracy protected the right of  expression the right the freedom of  expression and the freedom of thought he  established independent institutions  with an independent judiciary  however there's a caveat to that  constitution 1964  states that the king has the right to  dissolve the parliament and appoint the  prime minister under the current  situation it seems that  the  supreme leader of the taliban the emir  al-mu'min the commander of the faithful  will ultimately have the final say he  could be the one to fire the government  and point in your parliament  dissolve the parliament sorry or decide  that this provision is in conflict with  the sharia law and therefore it has to  be  it has to be changed  well let's now speak to jennifer  mutavashvili she's a non-resident senior  fellow at the atlantic council's eurasia  center focusing on central asia and  afghanistan she joins us now from  pittsburgh jennifer this is a  constitution that grants women the right  to vote but given that the taliban says  they'll eliminate any elements that they  disagree with what do you think it's  actually going to look like  don't think that they are implementing  this constitution to give women the  rights to vote and they've been very  clear that they don't think that  democracy is the way forward uh they're  implementing this constitution to get  political recognition simply uh to  establish a political order but without  a constitution it would be very hard for  them to have a government so this  constitution becomes very important for  them instrumentally to get that kind of  international recognition and that  they've said that they'll be able to  cherry-pick the parts of the  constitution that they believe  contradict  contradict their rule and they've been  pretty clear about democracy as not  being um symbolic of where they think  the country needs to go well there have  been a lot of promises that taliban  ruled this time round would take a more  inclusive form but there are obviously  still no women in the caretaker  government does this move as you suggest  get them more of the legitimacy that  they crave in the eyes of the world or  does that potentially backfire  i think it gives them a framework a  framework for governance it creates you  know as the correspondent just said as  the story from kabul said it gives the  head of the taliban the right sort of  the power of a king and he can then  allow the government to create laws and  dismiss laws as he sees fit so it really  gives authoritarian power this you know  did create a constitutional monarchy it  wasn't a democracy so um you know i  don't think it it it does much in terms  of changing much on the ground uh but it  does give a framework for laws and i  think this is just an incremental step  that they're trying to to use to get  this recognition  sure if they are cherry-picking parts of  the constitution then who right now  decides what to keep and what to get rid  of  it's the senior leadership and they're  using this as a bargaining chip with the  international community so they're using  gender rights they're using uh  inclusivity is something that they can  bargain with with the international  community in order to get aid this is a  very dangerous game that they're playing  uh because on the one hand they're  they're selecting this constitution not  the 2004 constitution they're going back  to this constitution because they say  that the 2004 constitution was created  by the puppets the puppet government it  was created by the international by the  united states and foreigners so the 1964  constitution in their eyes is more pure  the danger with using women's rights and  inclusivity is bargaining chips to get  international aid is the taliban are  then going to fall prey to the same trap  that they accuse the ghani government of  they're positioning themselves before  international donors and changing their  position based on what aid that they can  receive this creates a huge opening for  groups like isis and others who will  then accuse the taliban of becoming  puppets to the foreigners because  they're using this to get leverage for  aid well jennifer there is a commission  that was meant to be formed next year i  believe to draft a new constitution how  does that all fit into this process  could we potentially see that entire  constitutional process abandoned after  this happens  no this is actually an old play in fact  the united states adopted this 1964  constitution in 2001 right after the  taliban government collapsed the  previous taliban government and so for  three years the 1964 constitution was  used as the constitutional framework in  afghanistan before the 2004 constitution  was established and what that does is it  allows the framework of government to  continue it allows the center to  continue appoint ministers continue to  point to provincial governors and  district governors the same way that the  1964 constitution did in fact the 2004  constitution was hardly a departure from  this 1964 constitution there were many  things uh that these two constitutions  had uh  they were quite similar to one another  and that they were both very  authoritarian in nature so this is not a  good sign uh going into the next few  months  very interesting indeed jennifer mutava  villi there a non-resident senior fellow  at the atlantic council's eurasia center  thanks so much for sharing your  expertise with us on al jazeera thanks  for joining us jennifer  thank you

